{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/425506-bloodline-childer-of-the-morrigan","id":425506,"title":"Bloodline: Childer of the Morrigan","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-02T12:08:00-08:00","id":425506,"title":"Bloodline: Childer of the Morrigan","content":"I originally went in search of this blood line and found the post by Griautis. I liked the translation from 1st edition to 2nd edition, but I felt it lacked something. That the weakness alone wasn't justified and it felt as though it weren't special. I added my own little twists, since a player was recently turned into this Bloodline, I wanted it to be fierce and lethal, with draw backs. I would be open to hearing any suggestions and comments about my idea. I wrote my own dirge and two devotions for this bloodline, as well as a new gift. I found them all to be fitting, but still would like to hear the communities opinion.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>Childer of Morrigan - </b></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">For background refer to Circle of the Crone book, pg. 172. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Bloodline Disciplines: </b><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Animalism, Protean, Resilience, Vigor</span></span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#232323\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Bloodline Bane: </b></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><b>Second Dirge: Conqueror</b></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">A conqueror exists to destroy and do anything to defeat foes. A conqueror will finish off an enemy and never leave their opponent standing.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#232323\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Single Willpower:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Defeat someone in combat who challenged you.</span></span></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">All Willpower:Needlessly kill someone who has been defeated.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:#232323\"><b>Depression: </b></span><span style=\"color:#232323\">Stormcrows suffer the same base affliction as their parent clan. Defeat, however, is not something a Morrigan makes peace with easily herself. Her dedication to the covenant is distilled in her blood. In addition to the afflictions inherent in her Gangrel blood, a Morrigan is subject to a terrible dread of the soul. Stormcrows suffer from a permanent, unresolvable persistent Depression Condition. In addition to normal triggers, the following circumstances also prompt a Resolve + Composure roll to see if the Morrigan suffers the effects:</span></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#232323\">\u2022 <span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Defeat in battle: The Morrigan is a part of any battle in which Acolyte losses (including ghouls, Retainers and the like) outnumber the opponent\u2019s losses. In some cases, losses may not even be necessary, if the number of injured or routed Acolytes is very high or if the persons hurt or slain are very close to the character.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#232323\">\u2022 <span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Wasted effort: The Morrigan spends a Willpower point to augment any trait or dice pool outside of combat yet fails at the action she was attempting. A Morrigan shouldn\u2019t devote herself so utterly to anything but the defense and safety of her fellow Acolytes.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#232323\">\u2022 <span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Disfavor: The Morrigan\u2019s Covenant Status: Circle of the Crone is reduced to zero dots. Each night that a Morrigan spends out of favor with her people provokes her Depression. However, in this case, if the roll fails the character may not spend any Willpower points for the rest of the night.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:#232323\"><b>New Condition: </b></span><span style=\"color:#232323\">Depression</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:#232323\">If your character fails to achieve a goal (not just fails a roll, but fails to accomplish some personal, desired end such as getting a job or saving a friend\u2019s life), he might go into a bout of depression for the remainder of the scene. A dramatic failure that occurs in any activity might also bring on a bout of depression. Regardless of the circumstances, make a reflexive Resolve + Composure roll. If the roll fails, your character loses one Willpower point and cannot spend any Willpower points for the remainder of the scene. (</span><span style=\"color:#232323\"><i>Resolution:</i></span><span style=\"color:#232323\"><i>The character is healed of Depression (Impossible for a Childer of the Morrigan)</i></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Bloodline Gift: </b></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><b>Inherent Ritual Power: </b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\">Every Childer of the Morrigan may perform Touch of the Morrigan Cruac ritual as an Instant action, even if the Stormcrow has no dots in Cr\u00faac herself. The roll to activate this ritual is therefore simply Manipulation + Occult, with a cost of 1 Vitae as usual. If learned in this way, the ritual becomes an instant, rather than an extended, action to cast \u2014 however many successes the Morrigan earns on a single roll is what the ritual power deals in damage. </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\"><i><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\">(Touch of the Morrigan: </span><span style=\"color:#231f20\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\">Level-Three Cr\u00faac Ritual)</span></span></i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><i><span style=\"color:#231f20\">The caster performs this ritual (Manipulation + Occult + Cr\u00faac is rolled) and channels his righteous ire into a tangible force. If the performance roll is successful, the user\u2019s mere touch becomes deadly. The sorcerer must then touch a subject with his open palm. (See \u201cTouching an Opponent,\u201d p. 157 of the </span><span style=\"color:#231f20\">World of Darkness Rulebook</span><span style=\"color:#231f20\">.) Contact inflicts an amount of lethal damage equal to the number of successes gained on the activation roll. (The power cannot be delivered through a punch or other unarmed close-combat attack.) This harm can be delivered only once per performance of the ritual, and the user\u2019s touch has the potential to inflict harm for one hour for every success gained on the activation roll. If that period of time passes without a touch being made, the power fades. The mark made by contact is physically manifest in accordance with its severity. A Touch of the Morrigan that inflicts one point of damage looks like a minor scar or livid bruise, while one that delivers five points of damage leaves the subject almost entirely blackened and charred looking. The visible injury fades as the damage is healed. This power affects only vampires, ghouls and other supernatural creatures. It seems that Kindred cannot inflict their viciousness on mortals in this manner.</span></i></span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">The Crow:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Protean 3</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">The Morrigan may take the Carrion-Crow in addition to the other 3 forms allowed.</span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Devotions:</b></span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><b>Murder of Crows \u2013 </b>Animalism 3, Protean 5</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\">While using Primeval Miasma the Morrigan may choose to become a murder of crows instead of mist.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><b>Call of Crows \u2013 </b>Animalism 3, Vigor 2 and/or Resilience 2</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Cost: None (1 Vitae)</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Action: Relexive</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">When activating Summon the Hunt and specifies crows, the Morrigan infuses her blood with Vigor and/or Resilience to activate Call of Crows. When the crows consume the Morrigan's blood they gain a dot of Vigor and/or Resilience and will obey the vampire's command as written for Summon the Hunt. (When activating Vigor and Resilience the vampire must spend 1 Vitae.)</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><b>Brutal Killer</b> \u2013 Protean 4, Vigor 4</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Cost: 2 Vitae</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">Action: Reflexive</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Andalus, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\">While using Unnatural Aspect, activate Brutal Killer to do aggravated damage on next brawl attack.</span> </span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4812,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4812-karhu_fueryon","user":"karhu_fueryon","date":". 03-03-2015, 07:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4812-karhu_fueryon","name":"karhu_fueryon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4812_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-03T00:50:57-08:00","id":426266,"title":"","content":"It seems all good to me. Except for a second Dirge part... that's not a bane, that's an additional gift.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B5_EJSgV1u4SOFFJc3M5VmFDWVk?resourcekey=0-GEErNnDbGqlKYgoiuGausw&amp;usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Bloodline conversions</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1IbeKfqhWuG8jPN9vpGmDo0VY9n9-4ejxvRzfJRoir4k/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My House rules</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/294-griautis","name":"Griautis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar294_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-03T16:36:42-08:00","id":427100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Griautis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/425506-bloodline-childer-of-the-morrigan?p=426266#post426266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems all good to me. Except for a second Dirge part... that's not a bane, that's an additional gift.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Why do you see it as a gift rather than a bane?<br /> <br /> I see it as a bane, because inevitably the second dirge is pushing a character towards becoming a monster. To me, that is not a gift.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4812-karhu_fueryon","name":"karh